Starting: *Write Section 1 tree: Entry Marker definition* (2/6)

---

## Section 1 — What an Entry Marker IS

```
ENTRY MARKER
│
├── WHAT IT IS
│   ├── The first thing in any file
│   ├── Sits before any code, HTML, or runnable content
│   ├── Replaces normal file header comments/formatting
│   └── It is NOT a comment — comments are dead, this is alive
│
├── WHAT "ALIVE" MEANS
│   ├── Structured the same way in every single .akilah file
│   ├── A script can open any .akilah file, read the top only,
│   │   and immediately know everything about that file
│   └── Does not require reading the rest of the file
│
├── WHAT IT REPLACES
│   ├── Normal file headers (// app.akilah — The Single Server...)
│   ├── Hardcoded section declarations
│   └── Any formatting that a human wrote for humans only
│
├── WHAT IT EXPECTS (ALWAYS THE SAME, REGARDLESS OF FILE TYPE)
│   ├── 1. A section number — clear, numbered entry ID
│   └── 2. YAML format within that section
│
├── WHAT IT FORCES
│   ├── Single caller only — designed for one entry point
│   └── Controlled access — stops uncontrolled entry
│
└── WHAT IT PROVIDES TO THE CALLER SCRIPT IMMEDIATELY
    ├── Type of file being rendered
    ├── Detailed narrative description of what to do next
    └── Amount of content / what to expect inside
```

---

Starting: *Write Section 2 tree: YAML format inside marker* (3/6)

## Section 2 — The YAML Format Inside the Entry Marker

```
YAML BLOCK (inside the entry marker, always in this order)
│
├── PART 1 — NARRATIVE DESCRIPTION
│   ├── Plain language description of what this file is
│   ├── Sent back to the caller script
│   ├── Caller script sends it to the model
│   ├── Model reads it and knows what this file is WITHOUT reading the file
│   └── Purpose: eliminates guessing, eliminates hallucination about file purpose
│
├── PART 2 — CONFIGURATION CONTAINER
│   ├── Holds the actual content type for this file
│   │   ├── Could be: HTML, component, section, data block
│   │   └── Declared here so the caller knows the shape before opening anything
│   ├── Container can have components within it
│   │   ├── Components can be sections
│   │   ├── Sections can have data
│   │   └── Data can be changed or configured
│   └── Also points to WHERE the data is saved / WHO owns the data
│
├── PART 3 — ACTIONS / SCRIPTS
│   ├── What this container can DO
│   ├── Not hardcoded in the caller — declared here
│   ├── Each action is tied to the section it belongs to
│   │   └── Numbered and configured in the same section it is related to
│   └── Subsequent actions can also CREATE or EDIT
│       ├── New sections
│       ├── Subsections
│       └── Components within subsections
│
└── PART 4 — EXECUTION GATE
    ├── Is this ready to execute — yes or no
    ├── Done / approved / ready for main controller
    ├── Main controller is the ONLY thing that executes
    └── This removes the need to hardcode sections
        └── Because hardcoded sections go wrong or go outdated
```

---

Starting: *Write Section 3 tree: Security agent model* (4/6)

## Section 3 — The Security Agent Model (NOT a static directory)

```
THE SHIFT: Directory → Security Agent
│
├── WHAT THE DIRECTORY ANALOGY GOT WRONG
│   ├── A directory is static
│   ├── You walk in, you read it, you go find things yourself
│   └── Nobody is doing anything on your behalf
│
├── WHAT THE SECURITY AGENT ACTUALLY DOES
│   ├── Has a list of what the caller is allowed to do
│   ├── Provides the caller with their OPTIONS
│   │   └── Options come from what the file declares — not hardcoded
│   ├── Does the work ON BEHALF of the caller
│   │   ├── Caller does not go find things themselves
│   │   └── Agent retrieves it, presents it, executes it
│   └── Caller only interacts with the agent — never directly with the file
│
├── HOW OPTIONS ARE DETERMINED
│   ├── Agent reads the entry marker
│   ├── Entry marker declares what this file can do (Part 3 — actions)
│   ├── Agent returns those options to the caller
│   └── Caller picks one — agent executes it
│
└── THE STAGING LAYER (actions between files)
    ├── All actions created must first be submitted into a staging section
    ├── This staging section sits BETWEEN any files involved
    ├── It shows:
    │   ├── What changes are happening
    │   └── What changes are GOING to happen
    └── Nothing moves directly file-to-file without passing through staging
```

---

Starting: *Write Section 4 tree: Staging gate + app.akilah as orchestrator* (5/6)

## Section 4 — `app.akilah` as Orchestrator + Staging Gate

```
app.akilah — THE ORCHESTRATOR
│
├── WHAT IT IS NOT
│   └── Not just a server. Not just a router.
│
├── WHAT IT IS
│   ├── The main controller
│   ├── The only thing that executes
│   └── The single point everything passes through
│
├── WHAT IT DOES FOR EVERY CALL (CLI, browser, assistant)
│   ├── 1. Identifies WHO is calling and what session they're in
│   ├── 2. Loads that user's connected capabilities
│   │   └── Not hardcoded — pulled from session controller
│   ├── 3. Returns a mediated view
│   │   ├── "Here is what you have"
│   │   ├── "Here is what you can do"
│   │   └── "Here is how it all connects"
│   ├── 4. Logs every request to staging
│   │   └── Nothing happens invisibly
│   └── 5. Gates execution
│       ├── You cannot just run something
│       ├── It stages it first
│       ├── Shows it for review
│       └── Only then executes
│
├── THE UI (webapp.akilah)
│   ├── Not static buttons
│   ├── Live reflection of what app.akilah loaded
│   ├── Change capabilities → UI changes
│   └── No hardcoding
│
├── THE ASSISTANT
│   ├── One single calling point
│   ├── One call to app.akilah
│   ├── app.akilah loads session + context + capabilities first
│   ├── Assistant gets handed the full state
│   └── Assistant does not guess — it knows
│
└── STAGING GATE (the layer nothing bypasses)
    ├── Every request → staging first
    ├── Every action between files → staging section between them
    ├── Shows what is happening and what is going to happen
    └── Approval required before anything is committed
```

